//@ run-pass
//@ requires: madaros
// Task 6 Step 2: wiring test for OCSP-into-x509_verify_chain. Reuses the
// exact same root/intermediate/leaf 3-level chain Task 5's
// ocsp_verify_good.sio/ocsp_verify_adversarial.sio generated and verified
// against (root.pem/inter.pem/leaf.pem, CN=chain-test.example,
// /tmp/chain_test_fixtures, generated 2026-08-24/25 -- NOT the same
// fixture as x509_chain_verify_positive.sio's own "Test Root CA T4" chain,
// which is a separate, independently-generated 3-level chain from the
// same day; Task 5's own OCSP responses were signed against ITS chain,
// so this test must use that same chain, not Task 4's). Both root and
// intermediate have `CA:TRUE` (intermediate additionally `pathlen:0`) and
// no keyUsage extension (key_usage_bits == 0, which chain_verify_path
// treats as "no restriction", matching x509_chain_verify_positive.sio's
// own fixture).
//
// Each case is its own function (not all three sharing one `main`) to
// stay under the per-function IR instruction budget documented in Task
// 5's completion report (IR_MAX_INSTRS 16384; a single function holding
// ~6.7KB of DER byte-array literals plus the OCSP-parsing call graph
// already overflowed it there) -- this test's own per-case byte budget
// (chain fixture ~2.4KB duplicated per case, plus one ~1.27-1.29KB OCSP
// response) is comparable in order of magnitude to that prior overflow,
// so each case gets its own function rather than risking the same limit.
//
// Real OCSP response generation commands (openssl OCSP responder against
// this exact chain) are recorded in Task 5's own test file headers and
// completion report; not repeated here.

